WEBVTT

00:00:09.208 --> 00:00:10.625
Hello there.

00:00:10.708 --> 00:00:11.958
Mary!

00:00:13.250 --> 00:00:15.625
Oh. Hello.

00:00:16.833 --> 00:00:19.125
Coming down in stair rods, isn't it?

00:00:19.208 --> 00:00:20.542
Oh...

00:00:21.208 --> 00:00:22.417
Yes.

00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:24.042
Mum...

00:00:28.083 --> 00:00:31.750
I hope you don't mind me asking,
but shouldn't you be at home?

00:00:31.792 --> 00:00:36.958
Oh, yes, I should. But... I haven't
quite worked out how to find one.

00:00:37.042 --> 00:00:40.125
- Where are your parents?
- They died when I was small.

00:00:40.208 --> 00:00:42.875
- Here we go.
- All I have left is my aunt.

00:00:42.958 --> 00:00:44.708
- And where's she?
- Darkest Peru.

00:00:44.750 --> 00:00:47.375
- In the home for retired bears.
- Yeah, course she is.

00:00:47.458 --> 00:00:48.958
How did you get here?

00:00:49.042 --> 00:00:51.000
- I stowed away. In a lifeboat.
- Cool!

00:00:51.083 --> 00:00:53.792
And ate marmalade.
Did you know bears like marmalade?

00:00:53.833 --> 00:00:56.208
I didn't even know bears could talk.

00:00:56.292 --> 00:00:57.958
Well, I'm a very rare bear.

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:00.167
There aren't many of us left.

00:01:00.250 --> 00:01:01.708
And what are you going to do now?

00:01:01.750 --> 00:01:05.208
Well, I thought I would probably
just sleep over there in that bin.

00:01:05.708 --> 00:01:07.667
That's the spirit. Anyway...

00:01:07.708 --> 00:01:10.333
- Dad!
- Why don't we find you some help?

00:01:10.458 --> 00:01:14.208
Oh, yes, please.
If you're sure it's no trouble?

00:01:14.542 --> 00:01:16.833
Of course it isn't. Is it, darling?

00:01:18.417 --> 00:01:19.625
Not at all.

00:01:24.250 --> 00:01:27.042
- Oh, yes, that is good.
- So, enn...

00:01:29.208 --> 00:01:30.875
- What's your name?
- Hmm?

00:01:30.958 --> 00:01:33.250
- Do bears even have names?
- Of course we do.

00:01:34.375 --> 00:01:36.083
My name is...

00:01:39.167 --> 00:01:40.250
Beg your pardon?

00:01:44.417 --> 00:01:45.708
Right.

00:01:47.500 --> 00:01:49.125
- Well, go on.
- Hmm?

00:01:49.208 --> 00:01:51.125
You try it.

00:01:51.208 --> 00:01:53.167
Back of the throat.

00:02:01.917 --> 00:02:05.625
Mr Brown, that is extremely rude.

00:02:07.750 --> 00:02:09.125
Oh, at last.

00:02:09.208 --> 00:02:10.792
Oh. Er, wait for me.

00:02:10.875 --> 00:02:12.792
Is someone coming to get him?

00:02:12.875 --> 00:02:15.000
Everyone's gone.
He'll have to come with us.

00:02:15.083 --> 00:02:16.458
- No way.
- Dad!

00:02:16.542 --> 00:02:18.958
- Stay where you are.
- He's so embarrassing.

00:02:19.083 --> 00:02:20.542
We can't leave him here.

00:02:20.625 --> 00:02:23.417
We can.
He's not our responsibility.

00:02:23.500 --> 00:02:26.042
He's a young bear
who needs our help, Henry.

00:02:26.167 --> 00:02:30.375
It's just for one night, until we can
find the right people to look after him.

00:02:30.542 --> 00:02:32.292
There you are.

00:02:37.292 --> 00:02:38.875
- OK.
- Yes!

00:02:38.917 --> 00:02:41.167
- Excuse me? Erm...
- Hello.

00:02:41.250 --> 00:02:44.083
I'm dreadfully sorry,
I don't actually know your name.

00:02:44.250 --> 00:02:48.042
Well, I've got a bear name, but it seems
to be rather hard to pronounce.

00:02:48.125 --> 00:02:49.167
That's not ripe.

00:02:49.250 --> 00:02:54.292
- Perhaps you'd like an English name.
- An English name? Like what?

00:03:02.917 --> 00:03:05.292
Oh, look, Henry, it's perfect.

00:03:05.375 --> 00:03:08.250
You want to call him Ketchup?
Ketchup the bear?

00:03:08.333 --> 00:03:10.458
- Paddington!
- Paddington?

00:03:10.583 --> 00:03:12.125
Paddington.

00:03:12.625 --> 00:03:15.042
Pa-dding-ton.

00:03:15.125 --> 00:03:16.792
Paddington!

00:03:16.875 --> 00:03:18.542
Sorry! I like it.

00:03:19.333 --> 00:03:23.708
Well, then, Paddington, how would you
like to come home with us?

00:03:28.333 --> 00:03:31.125
♪ London is the place for me

00:03:32.667 --> 00:03:35.958
♪ London, that lovely city

00:03:37.125 --> 00:03:39.167
♪ You could go to France or America

00:03:39.250 --> 00:03:41.458
♪ India, Asia or Australia

00:03:41.542 --> 00:03:44.917
♪ You're gonna come back to London City

00:03:50.583 --> 00:03:53.917
♪ London is the place for me

00:03:55.917 --> 00:03:59.458
Oh!
What sort of route do you call that?

00:03:59.583 --> 00:04:02.583
Well, the young bear
said it was his first time in London.

00:04:02.667 --> 00:04:04.333
I thought I'd show him the sights.

00:04:04.458 --> 00:04:06.583
- Should've charged morel
- Keep the change.

00:04:06.667 --> 00:04:09.000
Cheer up, mate.
Might never happen.

00:04:09.083 --> 00:04:11.167
Darling, have you got your keys?

00:04:21.792 --> 00:04:23.458
Come on, Paddington.

00:04:38.500 --> 00:04:40.208
Oh, yes.

00:04:43.417 --> 00:04:46.542
But this... this is wonderful!

00:04:48.125 --> 00:04:50.500
Do you know,
I was actually beginning to think

00:04:50.542 --> 00:04:53.750
nobody would give me a home,
but this... I That's ripe.

00:04:53.833 --> 00:04:56.667
This will suit me down to the ground.
Thank you very much.

00:04:56.708 --> 00:04:59.375
Er... we're not giving you a home.

00:04:59.917 --> 00:05:01.417
- Oh?
- It's just for the night.

00:05:01.917 --> 00:05:02.958
Oh.

00:05:03.042 --> 00:05:05.208
When a young person
comes to this country,

00:05:05.292 --> 00:05:08.417
I'm afraid they don't just move in
with the first people they meet.

00:05:08.458 --> 00:05:10.792
- No?
- You need a proper guardian.

00:05:10.875 --> 00:05:11.917
What's that?

00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:15.000
A grown-up who takes you
into their home and looks after you.

00:05:15.083 --> 00:05:16.333
Like you?

00:05:17.583 --> 00:05:20.708
Erm... Yes, well, I suppose so.

00:05:20.792 --> 00:05:22.625
But not you?

00:05:23.417 --> 00:05:25.000
No. We don't do that.

00:05:25.083 --> 00:05:28.792
- It's normally someone you know.
- But what if you don't know anybody?

00:05:28.875 --> 00:05:33.833
In that case, the authorities will house
you in some kind of government facility.

00:05:33.917 --> 00:05:35.750
What? Like an orphanage?

00:05:40.167 --> 00:05:42.125
No, no, no, not an orphanage.

00:05:42.208 --> 00:05:46.417
It would be more like an institution
for young souls

00:05:46.458 --> 00:05:50.708
whose parents have sadly passed on.

00:05:54.750 --> 00:05:55.875
Oh.

00:05:56.667 --> 00:05:59.708
- Well, what about the explorer?
- Who?

00:05:59.792 --> 00:06:03.208
The man who visited us in Darkest Peru.
He said we'd always be welcome.

00:06:03.292 --> 00:06:06.417
Well, what's his name? You could go now.

00:06:06.750 --> 00:06:08.792
Ooh, well, I don't know his human name.

00:06:09.792 --> 00:06:12.542
My uncle and aunt hadn't learnt
much English back then,

00:06:12.583 --> 00:06:15.000
so they always called him...

00:06:15.083 --> 00:06:18.333
There can't be that many explorers
who've been to Peru.

00:06:18.375 --> 00:06:19.708
Maybe we can find him.

00:06:19.792 --> 00:06:22.000
Without a name?
I wouldn't get your hopes up.

00:06:22.083 --> 00:06:25.083
Right, come on, you. Pyjamas.

00:06:26.292 --> 00:06:27.375
Jonathan!

00:06:28.083 --> 00:06:30.042
Walk.

00:06:32.208 --> 00:06:33.500
Don't worry, Paddington.

00:06:33.542 --> 00:06:35.583
- We'll find him.
- Oh, thank you.

00:06:35.708 --> 00:06:37.417
Let's look in the encyclopaedia,

00:06:37.458 --> 00:06:40.167
unless, of course,
you'd like to freshen up first.

00:06:40.250 --> 00:06:41.792
I beg your pardon?

00:06:41.875 --> 00:06:46.333
You know, use the facilities?
Most people do after a long journey.

00:06:46.542 --> 00:06:51.125
Oh. Well, if that's what most people do,
then I'd like to do that.

00:06:51.958 --> 00:06:54.708
- Top of the stairs.
- Top of the... Ah, stairs.

00:06:55.250 --> 00:06:57.875
OK. You're going that way. Got it.

00:06:58.542 --> 00:07:00.542
- Are you alright?
- All good.

00:07:01.542 --> 00:07:04.542
Hello. This is Henry Brown.32 Windsor Gardens.

00:07:04.625 --> 00:07:07.958
I just need to add something
to my home insurance policy.

00:07:08.708 --> 00:07:11.875
Well, what it is, is we have
a guest for the night, a bear,

00:07:11.958 --> 00:07:15.833
and I just needed some extra cover
for any da... Yes, a bear.

00:07:15.917 --> 00:07:17.250
No, a real one.

00:07:18.333 --> 00:07:20.292
About three foot six.

00:07:21.000 --> 00:07:22.917
Grizzly? Not particularly.

00:07:23.000 --> 00:07:25.583
Mind you, I haven't seen him
in the mornings.

00:07:25.708 --> 00:07:27.958
So, how much would that be?

00:07:28.042 --> 00:07:31.750
Batten down the hatches, young 'un.
There'll be a storm tonight.

00:07:31.833 --> 00:07:33.500
The radio said it was clearing up.

00:07:33.583 --> 00:07:36.792
Radio! I feel it in my knees.
My knees never lie.

00:07:36.875 --> 00:07:39.542
- Guess what, Mrs Bird! We found a bear!
- Uh-huh.

00:07:39.667 --> 00:07:42.500
- A real bear from Peru!
- That's nice, dear.

00:07:42.542 --> 00:07:44.042
You don't seem very surprised.

00:07:44.125 --> 00:07:47.458
I gave up being surprised when
they came up with the microwave oven.

00:07:47.583 --> 00:07:49.125
And I still don't trust you.

